<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Chengjia</h1><p class="st-meta">25 CE – 36 CE · capital Chengdu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Sichuan empire of Gongsun Shu, the self-styled White Emperor, who held out against the restored Han for eleven years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongsun-shu">Gongsun Shu</a> was a governor in Shu (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>) under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/xin-dynasty">Xin dynasty</a>. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xin-dynasty">Xin</a> collapsed he seized the region, took the title King of Shu and in 25 proclaimed himself emperor of Chengjia, with his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>, the same year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-guangwu-of-han">Liu Xiu</a> restored the Han.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Protected by mountains and supplied by the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> plain, Chengjia attracted officials fleeing the chaos in the north. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongsun-shu">Gongsun Shu</a> called himself the White Emperor, after the element of metal, which gave the name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baidicheng">Baidicheng</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-gorges">Three Gorges</a>, and issued iron coins. His rule was cautious and defensive.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Having conquered the north-west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-guangwu-of-han">Emperor Guangwu</a> turned on Shu: 'having got Long, one longs for Shu.' His generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cen-peng">Cen Peng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-han-general">Wu Han</a> advanced up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, and in 36 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongsun-shu">Gongsun Shu</a> was mortally wounded outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>, which Wu Han then sacked.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 25 CE – 36 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:60.20%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>25 CE – 36 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chengdu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gongsun Shu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chengjia (Gongsun Shu)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=30&amp;focus=chengjia" aria-label="Chengjia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7376 1059 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chengjia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7696 1315L7769 1191"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7696 1315h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7696 1315h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chengdu. <a href="/atlas?year=30&amp;focus=chengjia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

